{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/resources/skills/consensus-analysis/guide.md /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/resources/skills/consensus-analysis/references/behavior-facts.md; sed -n '1,230p' ../agent-source/doc.go; grep -n '\"'^func '\"' ../agent-source/raft.go\"","aggregated_output":"# Investigation and selection\n\nCandidate investigates a sourced question, including unknown requirements, protection premises or legal history. Obligation fixes an applicable responsibility without presuming violation; Check supplies inputs, history and observations. Missing authority calls for contract investigation, missing history for source work or conditional exploration. Neither gap bars a Candidate or licenses an invented obligation.\n\nFirst explain both core relationships from activity_roles and their connection in AuditSpec.core_overview. Follow representative work through owners, qualified support, aggregation, decision, propagation and consumption; explain acquisition, change and loss of context/authority and handling of old requests, replies, pending support and established decisions. Read the supporting membership, history, recovery and completion mechanisms needed for this backbone. Trace callers and callees, important rejection, retry and asynchronous paths; deeper variants may remain open.\n\nSave early discrepancies as sourced Surfaces or specific unknowns even when no obligation is ready. Distinguish open_details from core_gaps: unexplained formation, authority transition or old-work handling breaks readiness; a usable overview can retain local anomalies. Only a preconfigured directed_question permits a proportionate local boundary first; activity_focus does not. A usable map and first Candidate may share a product. Unmapped source work and exploration need no fabricated Fact.\n\nUse behavior-facts.md for implementation meaning. Select a principal Fact lifecycle with a sourced A1/A2 connection; a question need not span both. Direct behavior_ids establish, consume, invalidate or reinterpret it; supporting_behavior_ids explain separate history/context/consequence support. Cross-activity effects establish actual relationships without relabeling support Activities. Names and protocol labels establish neither relevance nor responsibility.\n\nUse question, counterevidence, unknowns, importance and trigger_rationale to identify what remains unexplained, what existing protection does establish, and what the next action will distinguish. A concise sourced difference suffices; no ranking, mandatory alternative list or confidence threshold. Compare contract reading, smaller exploration and another known relationship before committing to a costly test. “This input has not been run” alone is not a discriminator. An empty unknowns list does not disqualify a check whose earlier investigation established its value.\n\nWhen the actual mechanism explains the suspicion and no important execution premise remains, use explained or sourced feedback and reselect without manufacturing a Unit. An explicit guard with unresolved ownership, timing, producer or context instead calls for investigation of that premise. A negative result should identify the plausible explanation it excluded and independent premises left open; distinguish it from execution corroborating an already-explained protection. Neither settles the entire Fact or becomes a reason to expand input variants indefinitely. Keep one requirement's independent scenarios together; a genuinely different responsibility gets its own question.\n\nConditional differences with unclear contracts remain exploration or local pause. Once responsibility is grounded, obligation and construction may be separate products. Reuse helpers and legal prefixes only after checking their entry, caller duties, completion order and fault assumptions for this question. A convenient driver must not exclude permitted overlap that supplied the discriminator, nor assume a looser ordering is legal because it produces a difference. Fixed checks must still exercise the investigated premise; a narrower child cannot settle its parent. Use existing F2/F3 when requirement or scope changes. Finish necessary technical repair and review of accepted work; switching questions preserves its debt.\n\nPrioritize consensus safety and explicitly scoped progress duties. Distinguish a sourced numeric/event deadline, implementation-owned automatic continuation without a numeric deadline, and later work explicitly owed by the caller. No numeric deadline does not preclude investigating lost continuation triggers; the word automatic alone supplies no obligation. A possible later event in a comment is not a guarantee that callers produce it. Independently grounded eligibility, accounting, wakeup or correlation duties can be resolved without a client endpoint or global consequence. Shared facts and helpers transfer understanding, not results.\n\nAt handoffs identify the answered proposition, supporting sources/execution and independent remaining question in feedback. At the next substantive product already updating the relevant Behavior, Fact or overview, or preparing a Candidate that relies on that knowledge, replace the answered original sentence with its sourced conditional relationship and remove obsolete execution/review reminders. Preserve independent unknowns and historical inputs. Consult the actual answer and assessment; an understanding label or exact reference does not establish resolution. Correct misleading explanations before reuse, without a dedicated cleanup turn or unrelated whole-map sweep. Source reading and exploration may continue from feedback; near expiry save minimal complete learning. Use behavior-facts.md for field ownership and tasks/audit.md for product references.\n\nOpen runs continue investigation until controller time/call or actual interruption boundaries. Empty pending_work or disposed registered questions does not exhaust the frontier: use the overview, other consumers/contexts, Surfaces and unread paths. Execution, Unit, review or revision limits restrict those actions, not remaining authorized source work. Near expiry take a smaller informative step; do not reset budgets, repeat empty work or sleep to fill time. Only a preconfigured finite directed task may complete early. Actual cancellation, safety refusal, permission loss and unrecoverable service/tool failure still stop.\n# Conditions, effects and semantic facts\n\nDescribe sourced owners, triggers, guards, state changes, effects and asynchronous boundaries. For a relevant relationship already read, identify who establishes the fact under which conditions and what its consumer actually requires. Include other read establishment, invalidation or reinterpretation paths; unread paths remain unknown or sourced Surfaces.\n\nShared updates do not imply shared follow-up effects. When one entry establishes a fact and schedules a consumer but another only establishes the fact, preserve that distinction in trigger, important_branches, writes, external_effects or async_boundaries. Do not union effects into an unconditional guarantee. Distinguish possible effects, conditional scheduling and guaranteed completion. A Behavior can cover multiple entries when conditions remain clear; split minimally when owners, completion boundaries or meanings cannot be expressed accurately together. Do not enumerate every branch or duplicate a shared Fact.\n\nFact states the assertion actually established for its identity and validity_context, not the strongest effect seen on any path. Reporting, internal accounting, notification and completed external work are distinct. Trace relevant guard inputs to their producer, update point, owner and completion duty instead of trusting a variable name. Conversely, a missing local field is not a missing guarantee when dispatch, admission, serialization or reset supplies it. Assess the composed producer/consumer protection, not each structure in isolation. Absent direct action is a question: inspect alternative consumers, delayed validation, shared scheduling and recovery for the same object and history before alleging a defect.\n\nUse produces_fact_ids/consumes_fact_ids for established relationships needed by later questions; reverse indexes are derived. No name-based edges, mandatory edges, branch fields or completeness scores. Reference validation proves structure, not equivalent path meaning. Guards and observed differences do not supply normative obligations without applicable sources and reasoning.\n\nUse existing map_changes at natural handoffs. Correcting an overstrong abstraction challenges the specific Candidate premise that depended on it; independent results retain their original basis. Use preserves only when requirements, dependencies and observation scope actually remain applicable. Meaning, identity, context and dependency changes cannot masquerade as clarification. No change means no version; keep history and counterevidence.\n\nKeep Fact meaning, identity and validity_context about the assertion established, not the full explanation of a Candidate. Put newly understood producer guards, retry branches, notifications and completion duties in Behavior, including field changes that schedule no follow-up. At a related handoff remove only the answered unknown and retain independent gaps; an explained Candidate needs no new challenge merely to restate its protection. If that protection or the Fact's actual context changes, attribute the challenge even when the Candidate was explained. Candidate/feedback owns the scoped disposition, Scope owns exclusions, and CheckRun/assessment owns execution/review progress. Name unresolved semantic premises instead of process reminders. Surface.reason is a reading question; mapped means association, not resolution. Map and map_changes source_ids cite Material ranges; experiment identities stay in feedback and fixed artifacts.\n// Copyright 2015 The etcd Authors\n//\n// Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (the \"License\");\n// you may not use this file except in compliance with the License.\n// You may obtain a copy of the License at\n//\n//     http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\n//\n// Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software\n// distributed under the License is distributed on an \"AS IS\" BASIS,\n// WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.\n// See the License for the specific language governing permissions and\n// limitations under the License.\n\n/*\nPackage raft sends and receives messages in the Protocol Buffer format\ndefined in the raftpb package.\n\nRaft is a protocol with which a cluster of nodes can maintain a replicated state machine.\nThe state machine is kept in sync through the use of a replicated log.\nFor more details on Raft, see \"In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm\"\n(https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf) by Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout.\n\nA simple example application, _raftexample_, is also available to help illustrate\nhow to use this package in practice:\nhttps://github.com/etcd-io/etcd/tree/main/contrib/raftexample\n\n# Usage\n\nThe primary object in raft is a Node. You either start a Node from scratch\nusing raft.StartNode or start a Node from some initial state using raft.RestartNode.\n\nTo start a node from scratch:\n\n\tstorage := raft.NewMemoryStorage()\n\tc := &Config{\n\t  ID:              0x01,\n\t  ElectionTick:    10,\n\t  HeartbeatTick:   1,\n\t  Storage:         storage,\n\t  MaxSizePerMsg:   4096,\n\t  MaxInflightMsgs: 256,\n\t}\n\tn := raft.StartNode(c, []raft.Peer{{ID: 0x02}, {ID: 0x03}})\n\nTo restart a node from previous state:\n\n\tstorage := raft.NewMemoryStorage()\n\n\t// recover the in-memory storage from persistent\n\t// snapshot, state and entries.\n\tstorage.ApplySnapshot(snapshot)\n\tstorage.SetHardState(state)\n\tstorage.Append(entries)\n\n\tc := &Config{\n\t  ID:              0x01,\n\t  ElectionTick:    10,\n\t  HeartbeatTick:   1,\n\t  Storage:         storage,\n\t  MaxSizePerMsg:   4096,\n\t  MaxInflightMsgs: 256,\n\t}\n\n\t// restart raft without peer information.\n\t// peer information is already included in the storage.\n\tn := raft.RestartNode(c)\n\nNow that you are holding onto a Node you have a few responsibilities:\n\nFirst, you must read from the Node.Ready() channel and process the updates\nit contains. These steps may be performed in parallel, except as noted in step\n2.\n\n1. Write HardState, Entries, and Snapshot to persistent storage if they are\nnot empty. Note that when writing an Entry with Index i, any\npreviously-persisted entries with Index >= i must be discarded.\n\n2. Send all Messages to the nodes named in the To field. It is important that\nno messages be sent until the latest HardState has been persisted to disk,\nand all Entries written by any previous Ready batch (Messages may be sent while\nentries from the same batch are being persisted). To reduce the I/O latency, an\noptimization can be applied to make leader write to disk in parallel with its\nfollowers (as explained at section 10.2.1 in Raft thesis). If any Message has type\nMsgSnap, call Node.ReportSnapshot() after it has been sent (these messages may be\nlarge).\n\nNote: Marshalling messages is not thread-safe; it is important that you\nmake sure that no new entries are persisted while marshalling.\nThe easiest way to achieve this is to serialize the messages directly inside\nyour main raft loop.\n\n3. Apply Snapshot (if any) and CommittedEntries to the state machine.\nIf any committed Entry has Type EntryConfChange, call Node.ApplyConfChange()\nto apply it to the node. The configuration change may be cancelled at this point\nby setting the NodeID field to zero before calling ApplyConfChange\n(but ApplyConfChange must be called one way or the other, and the decision to cancel\nmust be based solely on the state machine and not external information such as\nthe observed health of the node).\n\n4. Call Node.Advance() to signal readiness for the next batch of updates.\nThis may be done at any time after step 1, although all updates must be processed\nin the order they were returned by Ready.\n\nSecond, all persisted log entries must be made available via an\nimplementation of the Storage interface. The provided MemoryStorage\ntype can be used for this (if you repopulate its state upon a\nrestart), or you can supply your own disk-backed implementation.\n\nThird, when you receive a message from another node, pass it to Node.Step:\n\n\tfunc recvRaftRPC(ctx context.Context, m raftpb.Message) {\n\t\tn.Step(ctx, m)\n\t}\n\nFinally, you need to call Node.Tick() at regular intervals (probably\nvia a time.Ticker). Raft has two important timeouts: heartbeat and the\nelection timeout. However, internally to the raft package time is\nrepresented by an abstract \"tick\".\n\nThe total state machine handling loop will look something like this:\n\n\tfor {\n\t  select {\n\t  case <-s.Ticker:\n\t    n.Tick()\n\t  case rd := <-s.Node.Ready():\n\t    saveToStorage(rd.State, rd.Entries, rd.Snapshot)\n\t    send(rd.Messages)\n\t    if !raft.IsEmptySnap(rd.Snapshot) {\n\t      processSnapshot(rd.Snapshot)\n\t    }\n\t    for _, entry := range rd.CommittedEntries {\n\t      process(entry)\n\t      if entry.Type == raftpb.EntryConfChange {\n\t        var cc raftpb.ConfChange\n\t        cc.Unmarshal(entry.Data)\n\t        s.Node.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n\t      }\n\t    }\n\t    s.Node.Advance()\n\t  case <-s.done:\n\t    return\n\t  }\n\t}\n\nTo propose changes to the state machine from your node take your application\ndata, serialize it into a byte slice and call:\n\n\tn.Propose(ctx, data)\n\nIf the proposal is committed, data will appear in committed entries with type\nraftpb.EntryNormal. There is no guarantee that a proposed command will be\ncommitted; you may have to re-propose after a timeout.\n\nTo add or remove a node in a cluster, build ConfChange struct 'cc' and call:\n\n\tn.ProposeConfChange(ctx, cc)\n\nAfter config change is committed, some committed entry with type\nraftpb.EntryConfChange will be returned. You must apply it to node through:\n\n\tvar cc raftpb.ConfChange\n\tcc.Unmarshal(data)\n\tn.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n\nNote: An ID represents a unique node in a cluster for all time. A\ngiven ID MUST be used only once even if the old node has been removed.\nThis means that for example IP addresses make poor node IDs since they\nmay be reused. Node IDs must be non-zero.\n\n# Usage with Asynchronous Storage Writes\n\nThe library can be configured with an alternate interface for local storage\nwrites that can provide better performance in the presence of high proposal\nconcurrency by minimizing interference between proposals. This feature is called\nAsynchronousStorageWrites, and can be enabled using the flag on the Config\nstruct with the same name.\n\nWhen Asynchronous Storage Writes is enabled, the responsibility of code using\nthe library is different from what was presented above. Users still read from\nthe Node.Ready() channel. However, they process the updates it contains in a\ndifferent manner. Users no longer consult the HardState, Entries, and Snapshot\nfields (steps 1 and 3 above). They also no longer call Node.Advance() to\nindicate that they have processed all entries in the Ready (step 4 above).\nInstead, all local storage operations are also communicated through messages\npresent in the Ready.Message slice.\n\nThe local storage messages come in two flavors. The first flavor is log append\nmessages, which target a LocalAppendThread and carry Entries, HardState, and a\nSnapshot. The second flavor is entry application messages, which target a\nLocalApplyThread and carry CommittedEntries. Messages to the same target must be\nreliably processed in order. Messages to different targets can be processed in\nany order.\n\nEach local storage message carries a slice of response messages that must\ndelivered after the corresponding storage write has been completed. These\nresponses may target the same node or may target other nodes.\n\nWith Asynchronous Storage Writes enabled, the total state machine handling loop\nwill look something like this:\n\n\tfor {\n\t  select {\n\t  case <-s.Ticker:\n\t    n.Tick()\n\t  case rd := <-s.Node.Ready():\n\t    for _, m := range rd.Messages {\n\t      switch m.To {\n\t      case raft.LocalAppendThread:\n\t        toAppend <- m\n\t      case raft.LocalApplyThread:\n\t        toApply <-m\n\t      default:\n\t        sendOverNetwork(m)\n\t      }\n\t    }\n\t  case <-s.done:\n\t    return\n\t  }\n\t}\n\nUsage of Asynchronous Storage Writes will typically also contain a pair of\nstorage handler threads, one for log writes (append) and one for entry\napplication to the local state machine (apply). Those will look something like:\n\n\t// append thread\n\tgo func() {\n\t  for {\n\t    select {\n95:func (r *lockedRand) Intn(n int) int {\n119:func (st StateType) String() string {\n291:func (c *Config) validate() error {\n437:func newRaft(c *Config) *raft {\n498:func (r *raft) hasLeader() bool { return r.lead != None }\n500:func (r *raft) softState() SoftState { return SoftState{Lead: r.lead, RaftState: r.state} }\n502:func (r *raft) hardState() pb.HardState {\n512:func (r *raft) send(m pb.Message) {\n603:func (r *raft) sendAppend(to uint64) {\n616:func (r *raft) maybeSendAppend(to uint64, sendIfEmpty bool) bool {\n664:func (r *raft) maybeSendSnapshot(to uint64, pr *tracker.Progress) bool {\n692:func (r *raft) sendHeartbeat(to uint64, ctx []byte) {\n712:func (r *raft) bcastAppend() {\n722:func (r *raft) bcastHeartbeat() {\n731:func (r *raft) bcastHeartbeatWithCtx(ctx []byte) {\n740:func (r *raft) appliedTo(index uint64, size entryEncodingSize) {\n769:func (r *raft) appliedSnap(snap *pb.Snapshot) {\n778:func (r *raft) maybeCommit() bool {\n784:func (r *raft) reset(term uint64) {\n815:func (r *raft) appendEntry(es ...pb.Entry) (accepted bool) {\n850:func (r *raft) tickElection() {\n862:func (r *raft) tickHeartbeat() {\n891:func (r *raft) becomeFollower(term uint64, lead uint64) {\n902:func (r *raft) becomeCandidate() {\n917:func (r *raft) becomePreCandidate() {\n933:func (r *raft) becomeLeader() {\n973:func (r *raft) hup(t CampaignType) {\n995:func (r *raft) hasUnappliedConfChanges() bool {\n1025:func (r *raft) campaign(t CampaignType) {\n1075:func (r *raft) poll(id uint64, t pb.MessageType, v bool) (granted int, rejected int, result quorum.VoteResult) {\n1085:func (r *raft) Step(m pb.Message) error {\n1267:func stepLeader(r *raft, m pb.Message) error {\n1668:func stepCandidate(r *raft, m pb.Message) error {\n1713:func stepFollower(r *raft, m pb.Message) error {\n1777:func logSliceFromMsgApp(m *pb.Message) logSlice {\n1786:func (r *raft) handleAppendEntries(m pb.Message) {\n1830:func (r *raft) handleHeartbeat(m pb.Message) {\n1835:func (r *raft) handleSnapshot(m pb.Message) {\n1857:func (r *raft) restore(s pb.Snapshot) bool {\n1942:func (r *raft) promotable() bool {\n1947:func (r *raft) applyConfChange(cc pb.ConfChangeV2) pb.ConfState {\n1975:func (r *raft) switchToConfig(cfg tracker.Config, trk tracker.ProgressMap) pb.ConfState {\n2033:func (r *raft) loadState(state pb.HardState) {\n2045:func (r *raft) pastElectionTimeout() bool {\n2049:func (r *raft) resetRandomizedElectionTimeout() {\n2053:func (r *raft) sendTimeoutNow(to uint64) {\n2057:func (r *raft) abortLeaderTransfer() {\n2062:func (r *raft) committedEntryInCurrentTerm() bool {\n2070:func (r *raft) responseToReadIndexReq(req pb.Message, readIndex uint64) pb.Message {\n2094:func (r *raft) increaseUncommittedSize(ents []pb.Entry) bool {\n2112:func (r *raft) reduceUncommittedSize(s entryPayloadSize) {\n2123:func releasePendingReadIndexMessages(r *raft) {\n2142:func sendMsgReadIndexResponse(r *raft, m pb.Message) {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"I’m applying the supplied consensus-analysis guide. The caller contract distinguishes ordered storage completion from network delivery; I’ll trace how that distinction affects acknowledgment eligibility, term changes, and pending work.\n"}}
